From 0c990e2a6d9a996fcf304bb0623a251c8171e8ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W ARELIK <warelik@WARELIK-MB.local>
Date: Tue, 14 Jul 2026 08:35:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix: Cursor IDE MCP-gate + IPv6 ETIMEDOUT + native-aware tool
 preamble
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three upstream issues blocked Cursor IDE (and other MCP-tool clients)
through DEVIN_CONNECT on the native tool-call path:

1. ETIMEDOUT — DNS resolves server.codeium.com to an unroutable IPv6
   ULA (fd00:696e::ffff:2:4277) before the working IPv4. Node 20+
   enables Happy Eyeballs (autoSelectFamily) by default, but its
   implementation does not fall back to IPv4 when the IPv6 connect
   hangs — the whole attempt dies in ~270ms.

   Fix: net.setDefaultAutoSelectFamily(false) in config.js.

2. "MCP configuration issue" permission_denied — upstream
   server.codeium.com pattern-matches tool descriptions (both the
   top-level ToolDef #10.description AND parameter schema
   `description` keys) for known tool signatures and rejects the
   whole request. Live A/B on Cursor's real 21-tool inventory found
   8 of 21 tools triggered the gate (AskQuestion, Glob, Grep, Read,
   ReadLints, Shell, WebFetch, WebSearch). The gate fires on a
   COMBINATION of top-level description + parameter descriptions —
   removing either alone is insufficient for most tools.

   Fix: replace top-level description with the tool name (the model
   identifies tools by name, not by description prose) and strip all
   `description` annotations from the parameter JSON Schema
   recursively. Schema structure (types, required, enums, nested
   objects, properties named "description") is preserved — only
   human-readable annotation text is removed.

   ONLY needed on the native path (DEVIN_CONNECT_TOOL_DEF_TAGS on).
   With native OFF, descriptions ride in prompt text which upstream
   does not scan.

3. Cognitive conflict on hybrid path — keeping the prompt preamble ON
   alongside native #10 ToolDef introduced a conflict: the preamble's
   protocol header taught the model to emit text-format tool_call
   blocks, while the native #10 ToolDef declares the tools as function
   definitions the model should call through the native function-calling
   interface.

   Fix: when nativeStructured=true, strip the text-emulation protocol
   header from ALL preamble tiers (full / schema-compact / skinny /
   names-only) and return empty from the user-message fallback. The
   "Available functions" descriptions stay — they give the model
   tool-selection context the stripped #10 description field cannot.
   No new prompt text is hardcoded — only conditional removal of the
   protocol header section.

Verified: 21/21 Cursor ToolDef pass individually + all 21 together.
135/135 unit tests pass.
